        "original_id": "3bb06a67-039f-59a5-9851-74bfd797161a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Brendan Bracken, Winston Churchill, Adolf Hitler\n- Organizations: Bank of England, British Conservative Party\n- Places: United Kingdom, Ireland, Bournemouth, Paddington North\n- Roles: businessman, cabinet minister, central bank, parliamentarian\n- Events: World War II, Bournemouth by-election 1945, 1945 Labour landslide\n\nFacts:\n- The Bank of England was established in 1694 and is the second oldest central bank still operating after Sveriges Riksbank.\n- The Bank of England is the central bank of the United Kingdom and has served as a model for most modern central banks.\n- Brendan Bracken was born in Ireland on 15 February 1901 and became a businessman and a minister in the British Conservative cabinet.\n- Brendan Bracken is remembered for opposing the Bank of England's co-operation with Adolf Hitler.\n- During World War II, Brendan Bracken supported Winston Churchill's prosecution of the war against Adolf Hitler.\n- In the 1945 Labour landslide, Brendan Bracken lost the Paddington North seat to Labour.\n- In the 1945 Bournemouth by-election, Conservative candidate Brendan Bracken won the seat and was noted as a prominent supporter of Winston Churchill and a Conservative parliamentarian.\n- Brendan Bracken died on 8 August 1958.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d0389323-268f-5f63-8ab6-ede724a78d31",
        "text": "Page: Brendan Bracken. Sentence: Brendan Bracken, 1st Viscount Bracken, PC (15 February 1901 – 8 August 1958), was an Irish born businessman and a minister in the British Conservative cabinet.\nPage: Brendan Bracken. Sentence: He is best remembered for opposing the Bank of England's co-operation with Adolf Hitler, and for subsequently supporting Winston Churchill's prosecution of World War II against Hitler.\nPage: Bank of England. Sentence: The Bank of England, formally the Governor and Company of the Bank of England, is the central bank of the United Kingdom and the model on which most modern central banks have been based.\nPage: Bank of England. Sentence: Established in 1694, it is the second oldest central bank in operation today, after the Sveriges Riksbank.\nPage: Bournemouth by-election, 1945. Sentence: It was won by the Conservative candidate Brendan Bracken, who was a prominent supporter of Winston Churchill and Conservative parliamentarian who had lost his Paddington North seat to Labour in the 1945 Labour landslide.",
